  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8-3,\\,9,\\,6\u27e9.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\u27e8-3,\\,9,\\,6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83,\\,-9,\\,-6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \u27e83,\\,-9,\\,-6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,\\,-3,\\,-2\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
